  "latency_seconds": 4.322705068999994,
  "answer": "Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) works by allowing an adversary to implant malicious scripts into content that is served to web browsers. The goal of the attack is for the client-side browser to execute the script with the user's privilege level. This type of attack exploits vulnerabilities in programs that permit remote hosts to execute code and scripts. For instance, an attacker can inject a script into user-generated content on platforms like bulletin boards or blogs, which is then echoed back to other client machines, leading to potential redirection or execution of malicious actions [Source 1][Source 2]. \n\nTo successfully execute an XSS attack, the target client software must be capable of scripting communication from remote hosts, such as a JavaScript-enabled web browser [Source 3].",
